%I A397554 #53 Sep 26 2026 17:59:40
%S A397554 2,2,180,55440,122522400,130429015516800,2194123509006165668131200
%N A397554 a(n) is the least k such that sigma(k) > sigma(k-1)*n, where sigma(k) = A000203(k) is sum of divisors of k.
%C A397554 a(6) <= 130429015516800. - _Robert Israel_, Sep 09 2026
%C A397554 a(n) exists for all n since {sigma(k)/sigma(k-1)} is not bounded: First note that {sigma(k)/k} is not bounded. For every number k0, by Dirichlet's theorem on arithmetic progressions there exists a multiple k of k0 such that k-1 is prime, then sigma(k)/sigma(k-1) = sigma(k)/k >= sigma(k0)/k0. - _Jianing Song_, Sep 13 2026
%C A397554 a(8) <= 129536311027333194785285137864168507350612420728744415731028278389982742960. - _David A. Corneth_, Sep 21 2026
%e A397554 From _David A. Corneth_, Sep 21 2026: (Start)
%e A397554 a(7) = 2194123509006165668131200. Checking terms of A025487 that meet the more relaxed sigma(k) >= k*n we find 1970992304700453905270400, 2956488457050680857905600. The latter meets the requirements. If a(7) < 2956488457050680857905600 then it has the same prime signature as 1970992304700453905270400. Testing all* numbers <= 2956488457050680857905600 with the same prime signature as 1970992304700453905270400 we find 2194123509006165668131200.
%e A397554 * We may trim the search by cutting branches when sigma(k) < 7*k or candidates exceed 2956488457050680857905600 or 2194123509006165668131200 when found. (End)
%t A397554 a[n_]:=Module[{x=2},While[DivisorSigma[1,x]<=DivisorSigma[1,x-1]*n , x++]; x]; Array[a,4] (* _Stefano Spezia_, Jul 30 2026 *)
